Yeah. Your brain can generate a surprisingly strong endogenous state change from its own loops.

Memory, imagery, music, movement, breathing, expectation, self-talk, anticipation, social meaning, and interoception can all converge on the same reward/arousal systems. So you can get something that feels almost drug-like without an external drug:

imagery + expectation + bodily arousal + meaning + attention
→ amplified internal state

Placebo is part of that, but not in the sense of “fake.” Expectation can alter real physiology and perception. The interesting part is that the brain is both the generator and the audience.

You can also create feedback loops:

feel good → notice feeling good → interpret it as meaningful → feel even better → stronger imagery → stronger body response

That can become euphoria, flow, awe, confidence, runner’s high, religious ecstasy, sexual arousal, performance intensity, or just a very energized creative state depending on the inputs and interpretation.

The calibration piece is simply not to confuse intensity of experience with accuracy of belief. A feeling can be extremely real while the story attached to it is still just one hypothesis.

So yes: in a meaningful sense, people can learn to become very good at self-inducing states. The body is not only something that experiences the world; it is also something that can recursively experience its own predictions, memories, and imagination.